     `--skip-grant-tables' option to cause it to skip the normal grant
     table checks, then run *Note `mysql_upgrade': mysql-upgrade. For
     example:

          shell> mysqld --skip-grant-tables &
          shell> mysql_upgrade

     Then stop the server and restart it normally.

     You can specify other options on the *Note `mysqld': mysqld.
     command line if necessary.  Alternatively, if your installation is
     configured so that the server normally reads options from an
     option file, use the `--defaults-file' option to specify the file
     (enter each command on a single line):

          shell> mysqld --defaults-file=/usr/local/mysql/etc/my.cnf
                   --skip-grant-tables &
          shell> mysql_upgrade

     With the `--skip-grant-tables' option, the server does no password
     or privilege checking, so any client can connect and effectively
     have all privileges. For additional security, use the
     `--skip-networking' option as well to prevent remote clients from
     connecting.

