6.3.10 MySQL Enterprise Audit Log Plugin
----------------------------------------

* Menu:

* audit-log-plugin-installation::  Installing the Audit Log Plugin
* audit-log-security::           Audit Log Plugin Security Considerations
* audit-log-file::               The Audit Log File
* audit-log-plugin-logging-control::  Audit Log Plugin Logging Control
* audit-log-plugin-options-variables::  Audit Log Plugin Options and Variables
* audit-log-plugin-restrictions::  Audit Log Plugin Restrictions

*Note*:

MySQL Enterprise Audit is a commercial extension. To learn more about
commercial products (MySQL Enterprise Edition), see
http://www.mysql.com/products/.

As of MySQL 5.5.28, MySQL Enterprise Edition includes MySQL Enterprise
Audit, implemented using a server plugin named `audit_log'. MySQL
Enterprise Audit uses the open MySQL Audit API to enable standard,
policy-based monitoring and logging of connection and query activity
executed on specific MySQL servers. Designed to meet the Oracle audit
specification, MySQL Enterprise Audit provides an out of box, easy to
use auditing and compliance solution for applications that are governed
by both internal and external regulatory guidelines.

When installed, the audit plugin enables MySQL Server to produce a log
file containing an audit record of server activity. The log contents
include when clients connect and disconnect, and what actions they
perform while connected, such as which databases and tables they access.

After you install the plugin (see *Note
audit-log-plugin-installation::), it writes an audit log file. By
default, the file is named `audit.log' in the server data directory. To
change the name of the file, set the `audit_log_file' system variable at
server startup.

Audit log file contents are not encrypted. See *Note
audit-log-security::.

The audit log file is written in XML format, with auditable events
encoded as `<AUDIT_RECORD>' elements. For details on file format and
contents, see *Note audit-log-file::.

To control what information `audit_log' writes to its log file, set the
`audit_log_policy' system variable.  By default, this variable is set
to `ALL' (write all auditable events), but also permits values of
`LOGINS' or `QUERIES' to log only login or query events, or `NONE' to
disable logging.

For more information about controlling how logging occurs, see *Note
audit-log-plugin-logging-control::. For descriptions of the parameters
used to configure the audit log plugin, see *Note
audit-log-plugin-options-variables::.

The Performance Schema (see *Note performance-schema::) has
instrumentation for the audit log plugin. To identify the relevant
instruments, use this query:

     SELECT NAME FROM performance_schema.setup_instruments
     WHERE NAME LIKE '%/alog/%';


