14.4.1.1 Features of the InnoDB Storage Engine
..............................................

InnoDB in MySQL 5.5 contains several important new features:

   * *Note Fast index creation: add or drop indexes without copying the
     data: innodb-create-index.

   * *Note Data compression: shrink tables: (to significantly reduce
     storage and I/O)innodb-compression.

   * *Note New row format: fully off-page storage of long `BLOB':
     (`TEXT')innodb-row-format.

   * *Note File format management: protects upward and downward
     compatibility: innodb-file-format.

   * *Note `INFORMATION_SCHEMA' tables: information about compression
     and locking: innodb-information-schema.

   * *Note Performance and scalability enhancements:
     innodb-performance.:

        * *Note innodb-performance-latching::

        * *Note innodb-performance-use_sys_malloc::

        * *Note innodb-performance-change_buffering::

        * *Note innodb-performance-adaptive_hash_index::

        * *Note innodb-performance-thread_concurrency::

        * *Note innodb-performance-read_ahead::

        * *Note innodb-performance-multiple_io_threads::

        * *Note innodb-performance-aio-linux::

        * *Note innodb-performance-group_commit::

        * *Note innodb-performance-thread_io_rate::

        * *Note innodb-performance-adaptive_flushing::

        * *Note innodb-performance-pause_instruction::

        * *Note innodb-performance-spin_lock_polling::

        * *Note innodb-performance-midpoint_insertion::

        * *Note innodb-performance-recovery::

        * *Note innodb-performance-schema::

        * *Note innodb-multiple-buffer-pools::

        * *Note innodb-multiple-rollback-segments::

        * *Note innodb-improved-purge-scheduling::

        * *Note innodb-improved-log-sys-mutex::

        * *Note innodb-separate-flush-list-mutex::

        * *Note innodb-parameters-new-defaults::

   * *Note Other changes for flexibility: (ease of use and
     reliability)innodb-other-changes.:

        * *Note innodb-other-changes-file-formats::

        * *Note innodb-other-changes-dynamic-parameters::

        * *Note innodb-other-changes-truncate::

        * *Note innodb-other-changes-strict-mode::

        * *Note innodb-other-changes-statistics-estimation::

        * *Note innodb-other-changes-drop-index::

        * *Note innodb-other-changes-show-mutex::

        * *Note innodb-other-changes-readahead-counters::

* Upward and Downward Compatibility *

Note that the ability to use data compression and the new row format
require the use of a new InnoDB file format called Barracuda. The
previous file format, used by the built-in InnoDB in MySQL 5.1 and
earlier, is now called Antelope and does not support these features,
but does support the other features introduced with the InnoDB storage
engine.

The InnoDB storage engine is upward compatible from standard InnoDB as
built in to, and distributed with, MySQL. Existing databases can be
used with the InnoDB Storage Engine for MySQL. The new parameter
`innodb_file_format' can help protect upward and downward compatibility
between InnoDB versions and database files, allowing users to enable or
disable use of new features that can only be used with certain versions
of InnoDB.

InnoDB since version 5.0.21 has a safety feature that prevents it from
opening tables that are in an unknown format. However, the system
tablespace may contain references to new-format tables that confuse the
built-in InnoDB in MySQL 5.1 and earlier. These references are cleared
in a slow shutdown.

With previous versions of InnoDB, no error would be returned until you
try to access a table that is in a format `too new' for the software.
To provide early feedback, InnoDB 1.1 checks the system tablespace
before startup to ensure that the file format used in the database is
supported by the storage engine. See *Note
innodb-file-format-compatibility-checking:: for the details.


File: manual.info,  Node: innodb-introduction-obtaining,  Next: innodb-introduction-version,  Prev: innodb-introduction-features,  Up: innodb-introduction

14.4.1.2 Obtaining and Installing the InnoDB Storage Engine
...........................................................

Starting with MySQL 5.4.2, you do not need to do anything special to
get or install the most up-to-date InnoDB storage engine. From that
version forward, the InnoDB storage engine in the server is what was
formerly known as the InnoDB Plugin. Earlier versions of MySQL required
some extra build and configuration steps to get the Plugin-specific
features such as fast index creation and table compression.

Report any bugs in the InnoDB storage engine using the My Oracle
Support site (http://support.oracle.com). For general discussions about
InnoDB Storage Engine for MySQL, see
`http://forums.mysql.com/list.php?22'.


