22.6.8.14 Method `MySQLConnection.cursor(buffered=None, raw=None, cursor_class=None)'
.....................................................................................

This method returns a `MySQLCursor()' object, or a subclass of it
depending on the passed arguments.

When `buffered' is `True', the cursor fetches all rows after the
operation is executed. This is useful when queries return small result
sets. Setting `raw' skips the conversion from MySQL data types to
Python types when fetching rows. Raw is usually used when you want to
get better performance or you want to do the conversion yourself.

The `cursor_class' argument can be used to pass a class to use for
instantiating a new cursor. It must be a subclass of
`cursor.CursorBase'.

The returned object depends on the combination of the `buffered' and
`raw' arguments.

   * If not buffered and not raw: `cursor.MySQLCursor'

   * If buffered and not raw: `cursor.MySQLCursorBuffered'

   * If buffered and raw: `cursor.MySQLCursorBufferedRaw'

   * If not buffered and raw: `cursor.MySQLCursorRaw'

Returns a `CursorBase' instance.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-change-user,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-debug,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cursor,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.15 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_change_user(username='', password='', database='', charset=33)'
.....................................................................................................

Changes the user using `username' and `password'. It also causes the
specified `database' to become the default (current) database. It is
also possible to change the character set using the `charset' argument.

Returns a dictionary containing the OK packet information.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-debug,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-init-db,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-change-user,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.16 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_debug()'
..............................................

Instructs the server to write debugging information to the error log.
The connected user must have the `SUPER' privilege.

Returns a dictionary containing the OK packet information.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-init-db,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-ping,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-debug,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.17 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_init_db(database)'
........................................................

This method makes specified database the default (current) database. In
subsequent queries, this database is the default for table references
that include no explicit database qualifier.

Returns a dictionary containing the OK packet information.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-ping,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-process-info,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-init-db,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.18 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_ping()'
.............................................

Checks whether the connection to the server is working.

This method is not to be used directly. Use *Note ping():
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-ping.  or *Note is_connected():
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-is-connected.  instead.

Returns a dictionary containing the OK packet information.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-process-info,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-process-kill,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-ping,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.19 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_process_info()'
.....................................................

This method raises the NotSupportedError exception. Instead, use the
`SHOW PROCESSLIST' statement or query the tables found in the database
`INFORMATION_SCHEMA'.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-process-kill,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-query,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-process-info,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.20 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_process_kill(mysql_pid)'
..............................................................

Asks the server to kill the thread specified by `mysql_pid'. Although
still available, it is better to use the `KILL' SQL statement.

Returns a dictionary containing the OK packet information.

The following two lines have the same effect:


     >>> cnx.cmd_process_kill(123)
     >>> cnx.cmd_query('KILL 123')


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-query,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-query-iter,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-process-kill,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.21 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_query(statement)'
.......................................................

This method sends the given `statement' to the MySQL server and returns
a result. To send multiple statements, use the *Note cmd_query_iter():
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-query-iter.  method instead.

The returned dictionary contains information depending on what kind of
query was executed. If the query is a *Note `SELECT': select.
statement, the result contains information about columns. Other
statements return a dictionary containing OK or EOF packet information.

Errors received from the MySQL server are raised as exceptions.  An
`InterfaceError' is raised when multiple results are found.

Returns a dictionary.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-query-iter,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-quit,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-query,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.22 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_query_iter(statement)'
............................................................

Similar to the *Note cmd_query():
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-query.  method, but returns a
generator object to iterate through results. Use `cmd_query_iter()'
when sending multiple statements, and separate the statements with
semicolons.

The following example shows how to iterate through the results after
sending multiple statements:

     statement = 'SELECT 1; INSERT INTO t1 VALUES (); SELECT 2'
     for result in cnx.cmd_query_iter(statement):
       if 'columns' in result:
         columns = result['columns']
         rows = cnx.get_rows()
       else:
         # do something useful with INSERT result

Returns a generator object.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-quit,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-refresh,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-query-iter,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.23 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_quit()'
.............................................

This method sends a `QUIT' command to the MySQL server, closing the
current connection. Since there is no response from the MySQL server,
the packet that was sent is returned.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-refresh,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-shutdown,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-quit,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.24 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_refresh(options)'
.......................................................

This method flushes tables or caches, or resets replication server
information. The connected user must have the `RELOAD' privilege.

The `options' argument should be a bitmask value constructed using
constants from the `constants.RefreshOption' class.

For a list of options, see *Note connector-python-api-refreshoption::.

Example:


     >>> from mysql.connector import RefreshOption
     >>> refresh = RefreshOption.LOG | RefreshOption.THREADS
     >>> cnx.cmd_refresh(refresh)


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-shutdown,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-statistics,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-refresh,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.25 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_shutdown()'
.................................................

Asks the database server to shut down. The connected user must have the
`SHUTDOWN' privilege.

Returns a dictionary containing the OK packet information.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-statistics,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-disconnect,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-shutdown,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.26 Method `MySQLConnection.cmd_statistics()'
...................................................

Returns a dictionary containing information about the MySQL server
including uptime in seconds and the number of running threads,
questions, reloads, and open tables.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-disconnect,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-row,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-cmd-statistics,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.27 Method `MySQLConnection.disconnect()'
...............................................

This method tries to send a `QUIT' command and close the socket. It
raises no exceptions.

`MySQLConnection.close()' is a synonymous method name and more commonly
used.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-row,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-rows,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-disconnect,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.28 Method `MySQLConnection.get_row()'
............................................

This method retrieves the next row of a query result set, returning a
tuple.

The tuple returned by `get_row()' consists of:

   * The row as a tuple containing byte objects, or `None' when no more
     rows are available.

   * EOF packet information as a dictionary containing `status_flag' and
     `warning_count', or `None' when the row returned is not the last
     row.

The `get_row()' method is used by *Note MySQLCursor:
connector-python-api-mysqlcursor.  to fetch rows.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-rows,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-server-info,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-row,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.29 Method `MySQLConnection.get_rows(count=None)'
.......................................................

This method retrieves all or remaining rows of a query result set,
returning a tuple containing the rows as sequences and the EOF packet
information. The count argument can be used to obtain a given number of
rows. If count is not specified or is `None', all rows are retrieved.

The tuple returned by `get_rows()' consists of:

   * A list of tuples containing the row data as byte objects, or an
     empty list when no rows are available.

   * EOF packet information as a dictionary containing `status_flag' and
     `warning_count'.

An `InterfaceError' is raised when all rows have been retrieved.

*Note MySQLCursor: connector-python-api-mysqlcursor.  uses the
`get_rows()' method to fetch rows.

Returns a tuple.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-server-info,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-server-version,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-rows,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.30 Method `MySQLConnection.get_server_info()'
....................................................

This method returns the MySQL server information verbatim as a string,
for example `'5.6.11-log'', or `None' when not connected.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-server-version,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-is-connected,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-server-info,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.31 Method `MySQLConnection.get_server_version()'
.......................................................

This method returns the MySQL server version as a tuple, or `None' when
not connected.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-is-connected,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-isset-client-flag,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-server-version,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.32 Method `MySQLConnection.is_connected()'
.................................................

Reports whether the connection to MySQL Server is available.

This method checks whether the connection to MySQL is available using
the *Note ping(): connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-ping.  method,
but unlike `ping()', `is_connected()' returns `True' when the
connection is available, `False' otherwise.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-isset-client-flag,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-ping,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-is-connected,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.33 Method `MySQLConnection.isset_client_flag(flag)'
..........................................................

This method returns `True' if the client flag was set, `False'
otherwise.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-ping,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-reconnect,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-isset-client-flag,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.34 Method `MySQLConnection.ping(attempts=1, delay=0)'
............................................................

Check whether the connection to the MySQL server is still available.

When `reconnect' is set to `True', one or more attempts are made to try
to reconnect to the MySQL server using the `reconnect()' method. Use the
`delay' argument (seconds) if you want to wait between each retry.

When the connection is not available, an `InterfaceError' is raised.
Use the *Note is_connected():
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-is-connected.  method to check the
connection without raising an error.

Raises `InterfaceError' on errors.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-reconnect,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-rollback,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-ping,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.35 Method `MySQLConnection.reconnect(attempts=1, delay=0)'
.................................................................

Attempt to reconnect to the MySQL server.

The argument `attempts' specifies the number of times a reconnect is
tried. The `delay' argument is the number of seconds to wait between
each retry.

You might set the number of attempts higher and use a longer delay when
you expect the MySQL server to be down for maintenance, or when you
expect the network to be temporarily unavailable.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-rollback,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-set-charset-collation,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-reconnect,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.36 Method `MySQLConnection.rollback()'
.............................................

This method sends a `ROLLBACK' statement to the MySQL server, undoing
all data changes from the current transaction. By default,
Connector/Python does not autocommit, so it is possible to cancel
transactions when using transactional storage engines such as `InnoDB'.


     >>> cursor.execute("INSERT INTO employees (first_name) VALUES (%s)", ('Jane'))
     >>> cnx.rollback()

To commit modifications, see the *Note commit():
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-commit.  method.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-set-charset-collation,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-set-client-flags,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-rollback,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.37 Method `MySQLConnection.set_charset_collation(charset=None, collation=None)'
......................................................................................

This method sets the character set and collation to be used for the
current connection. The `charset' argument can be either the name of a
character set, or the numerical equivalent as defined in
`constants.CharacterSet'.

When `collation' is `None', the default collation for the character set
is used.

In the following example, we set the character set to `latin1' and the
collation to `latin1_swedish_ci' (the default collation for: `latin1'):


     >>> cnx = mysql.connector.connect(user='scott')
     >>> cnx.set_charset('latin1')

Specify a given collation as follows:


     >>> cnx = mysql.connector.connect(user='scott')
     >>> cnx.set_charset('latin1', 'latin1_general_ci')


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-set-client-flags,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-start-transaction,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-set-charset-collation,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.38 Method `MySQLConnection.set_client_flags(flags)'
..........................................................

This method sets the client flags to use when connecting to the MySQL
server, and returns the new value as an integer. The `flags' argument
can be either an integer or a sequence of valid client flag values (see
*Note connector-python-api-clientflag::).

If `flags' is a sequence, each item in the sequence sets the flag when
the value is positive or unsets it when negative. For example, to unset
`LONG_FLAG' and set the `FOUND_ROWS' flags:


     >>> from mysql.connector.constants import ClientFlag
     >>> cnx.set_client_flags([ClientFlag.FOUND_ROWS, -ClientFlag.LONG_FLAG])
     >>> cnx.reconnect()

Note that client flags are only set or used when connecting to the
MySQL server. It is therefore necessary to reconnect after making
changes.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-start-transaction,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-autocommit,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-set-client-flags,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.39 Method `MySQLConnection.start_transaction()'
......................................................

This method starts a transaction. It accepts accepts arguments
indicating whether to use a consistent snapshot and which transaction
isolation level to use:

     cnx.start_transaction(consistent_snapshot=BOOL,
                           isolation_level=LEVEL)

The default `consistent_snapshot' value is `False'. The default
`isolation_level' value is `None', and permitted values are `'READ
UNCOMMITTED'', `'READ COMMITTED'', `'REPEATABLE READ'', and
`'SERIALIZABLE''.

Invoking `start_transaction()' raises a `ProgrammingError' if invoked
while a transaction is currently in progress. This differs from
executing a *Note `START TRANSACTION': commit. SQL statement while a
transaction is in progress; the statement implicitly commits the current
transaction.

To determine whether a transaction is active for the connection, use
the the *Note in_transaction:
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-in-transaction.  property.

`start_transaction()' was added in MySQL Connector/Python 1.1.0.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-autocommit,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-charset-name,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-start-transaction,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.40 Property `MySQLConnection.autocommit'
...............................................

This property can be assigned a value of `True' or `False' to enable or
disable the autocommit feature of MySQL. The property can be invoked to
retrieve the current autocommit setting.

Note that autocommit is disabled by default when connecting through
Connector/Python. This can be enabled using the `autocommit' *Note
connection parameter: connector-python-connectargs.

When the autocommit is turned off, you must *Note commit:
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-commit.  transactions when using
transactional storage engines such as `InnoDB' or `NDBCluster'.


     >>> cnx.autocommit
     False
     >>> cnx.autocommit = True
     >>> cnx.autocommit
     True


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-charset-name,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-collation-name,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-autocommit,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.41 Property `MySQLConnection.charset_name'
.................................................

This property returns a string indicating which character set is used
for the connection, whether or not it is connected.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-collation-name,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-connection-id,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-charset-name,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.42 Property `MySQLConnection.collation_name'
...................................................

This property returns a string indicating which collation is used for
the connection, whether or not it is connected.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-connection-id,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-database,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-collation-name,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.43 Property `MySQLConnection.connection_id'
..................................................

This property returns the integer connection ID (thread ID or session
ID) for the current connection or `None' when not connected.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-database,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-warnings,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-connection-id,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.44 Property `MySQLConnection.database'
.............................................

This property sets the current (default) database by executing a `USE'
statement. The property can also be used to retrieve the current
database name.


     >>> cnx.database = 'test'
     >>> cnx.database = 'mysql'
     >>> cnx.database
     u'mysql'

Returns a string.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-warnings,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-in-transaction,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-database,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.45 Property `MySQLConnection.get_warnings'
.................................................

This property can be assigned a value of `True' or `False' to enable or
disable whether warnings should be fetched automatically. The default is
`False' (default). The property can be invoked to retrieve the current
warnings setting.

Fetching warnings automatically can be useful when debugging queries.
Cursors make warnings available through the method *Note
MySQLCursor.fetchwarnings():
connector-python-api-mysqlcursor-fetchwarnings.


     >>> cnx.get_warnings = True
     >>> cursor.execute('SELECT "a"+1')
     >>> cursor.fetchall()
     [(1.0,)]
     >>> cursor.fetchwarnings()
     [(u'Warning', 1292, u"Truncated incorrect DOUBLE value: 'a'")]

Returns `True' or `False'.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-in-transaction,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-raise-on-warnings,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-get-warnings,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.46 Property `MySQLConnection.in_transaction'
...................................................

This property returns `True' or `False' to indicate whether a
transaction is active for the connection. The value is `True'
regardless of whether you start a transaction using the *Note
`start_transaction()':
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-start-transaction.  API call or by
directly executing a SQL statement such as *Note `START TRANSACTION':
commit. or *Note `BEGIN': commit.


     >>> cnx.start_transaction()
     >>> cnx.in_transaction
     True
     >>> cnx.commit()
     >>> cnx.in_transaction
     False

`in_transaction' was added in MySQL Connector/Python 1.1.0.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-raise-on-warnings,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-server-host,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-in-transaction,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.47 Property `MySQLConnection.raise_on_warnings'
......................................................

This property can be assigned a value of `True' or `False' to enable or
disable whether warnings should raise exceptions. The default is
`False' (default). The property can be invoked to retrieve the current
exceptions setting.

Setting `raise_on_warnings' also sets `get_warnings' because warnings
need to be fetched so they can be raised as exceptions.

Note that you might always want to set the SQL mode if you would like
to have the MySQL server directly report warnings as errors (see *Note
connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-sql-mode::).  It is also good to
use transactional engines so transactions can be rolled back when
catching the exception.

Result sets needs to be fetched completely before any exception can be
raised. The following example shows the execution of a query that
produces a warning:


     >>> cnx.raise_on_warnings = True
     >>> cursor.execute('SELECT "a"+1')
     >>> cursor.fetchall()
     ..
     mysql.connector.errors.DataError: 1292: Truncated incorrect DOUBLE value: 'a'

Returns `True' or `False'.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-server-host,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-server-port,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-raise-on-warnings,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.48 Property `MySQLConnection.server_host'
................................................

This read-only property returns the host name or IP address used for
connecting to the MySQL server.

Returns a string.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-server-port,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-sql-mode,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-server-host,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.49 Property `MySQLConnection.server_port'
................................................

This read-only property returns the TCP/IP port used for connecting to
the MySQL server.

Returns an integer.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-sql-mode,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-time-zone,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-server-port,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.50 Property `MySQLConnection.sql_mode'
.............................................

This property is used to retrieve and set the SQL Modes for the current
connection. The value should be a list of different modes separated by
comma (","), or a sequence of modes, preferably using the
`constants.SQLMode' class.

To unset all modes, pass an empty string or an empty sequence.


     >>> cnx.sql_mode = 'TRADITIONAL,NO_ENGINE_SUBSTITUTION'
     >>> cnx.sql_mode.split(',')
     [u'STRICT_TRANS_TABLES', u'STRICT_ALL_TABLES', u'NO_ZERO_IN_DATE',
     u'NO_ZERO_DATE', u'ERROR_FOR_DIVISION_BY_ZERO', u'TRADITIONAL',
     u'NO_AUTO_CREATE_USER', u'NO_ENGINE_SUBSTITUTION']

     >>> from mysql.connector.constants import SQLMode
     >>> cnx.sql_mode = [ SQLMode.NO_ZERO_DATE, SQLMode.REAL_AS_FLOAT]
     >>> cnx.sql_mode

     u'REAL_AS_FLOAT,NO_ZERO_DATE'

Returns a string.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-time-zone,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-unix-socket,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-sql-mode,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.51 Property `MySQLConnection.time_zone'
..............................................

This property is used to set or retrieve the time zone session variable
for the current connection.


     >>> cnx.time_zone = '+00:00'
     >>> cur.execute('SELECT NOW()') ; cur.fetchone()
     (datetime.datetime(2012, 6, 15, 11, 24, 36),)
     >>> cnx.time_zone = '-09:00'
     >>> cur.execute('SELECT NOW()') ; cur.fetchone()
     (datetime.datetime(2012, 6, 15, 2, 24, 44),)
     >>> cnx.time_zone
     u'-09:00'

Returns a string.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-unix-socket,  Next: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-user,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-time-zone,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.52 Property `MySQLConnection.unix_socket'
................................................

This read-only property returns the Unix socket file for connecting to
the MySQL server.

Returns a string.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-user,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection-unix-socket,  Up: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection

22.6.8.53 Property `MySQLConnection.user'
.........................................

This read-only property returns the user name used for connecting to
the MySQL server.

Returns a string.


File: manual.info,  Node: connector-python-api-mysqlconnectionpool,  Next: connector-python-api-pooledmysqlconnection,  Prev: connector-python-api-mysqlconnection,  Up: connector-python-reference

22.6.8.54 Class `pooling.MySQLConnectionPool'
.............................................

* Menu:

