22.9.4.19 `MysqlndUhConnection::connect'
........................................

Copyright 1997-2012 the PHP Documentation Group.

   * `MysqlndUhConnection::connect'

     Open a new connection to the MySQL server

*Description*

public boolMysqlndUhConnection::connect

mysqlnd_connectionconnection

stringhost

stringuse"

stringpassword

stringdatabase

intport

stringsocket

intmysql_flags

Open a new connection to the MySQL server.

*Parameters*

  1. connection

     Mysqlnd connection handle. Do not modify!

  2. host

     Can be either a host name or an IP address. Passing the NULL value
     or the string "localhost" to this parameter, the local host is
     assumed. When possible, pipes will be used instead of the TCP/IP
     protocol.

  3. user

     The MySQL user name.

  4. password

     If not provided or

     NULL

     , the MySQL server will attempt to authenticate the user against
     those user records which have no password only.  This allows one
     username to be used with different permissions (depending on if a
     password as provided or not).

  5. database

     If provided will specify the default database to be used when
     performing queries.

  6. port

     Specifies the port number to attempt to connect to the MySQL
     server.

  7. socket

     Specifies the socket or named pipe that should be used. If

     NULL

     , mysqlnd will default to `/tmp/mysql.sock'.

  8. mysql_flags

     Connection options.

*Return Values*

Returns

TRUE

on success. Otherwise, returns

FALSE

*Examples*


     <?php
     class proxy extends MysqlndUhConnection {
      public function connect($res, $host, $user, $passwd, $db, $port, $socket, $mysql_flags) {
        printf("%s(%s)\n", __METHOD__, var_export(func_get_args(), true));
        $ret = parent::connect($res, $host, $user, $passwd, $db, $port, $socket, $mysql_flags);
        printf("%s returns %s\n", __METHOD__, var_export($ret, true));
        return $ret;
      }
     }
     mysqlnd_uh_set_connection_proxy(new proxy());

     $mysqli = new mysqli("localhost", "root", "", "test");
     ?>

The above example will output:


     proxy::connect(array (
       0 => NULL,
       1 => 'localhost',
       2 => 'root',
       3 => '',
       4 => 'test',
       5 => 3306,
       6 => NULL,
       7 => 131072,
     ))
     proxy::connect returns true

*See Also*

*Note `mysqlnd_uh_set_connection_proxy':
apis-php-function.mysqlnd-uh-set-connection-proxy.

`mysqli_connect'

`mysql_connect'


